    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Problems involving surfaces in contact with cohesion and friction are among the most difficult to solve because the boundary conditions on the contact surfaces as well as the contact response to the loading are not known a priori. That is, in many circumstances, fractures are partly or completely closed or, if open, may close under compression as the loading evolves and fracture tips propagate. Once a critical shear stress is exceeded at some point during the loading process, parts of closed fractures may begin to slip and later stick. For compaction bands, in addition to the unknown boundary conditions, specific relationships between contact forces and contact displacements may be required. In this paper we present a 2D boundary element code to model shear fractures and compaction bands. In the model, boundary conditions along the surfaces of a closed fracture are defined using a complementarity algorithm. According to our results this method increases accuracy, enforcing the contact constraints strictly, and avoids user defined parameters; reduces the computational time significantly by eliminating incremental, trial and error solution procedures; and vastly improves the convergence of the models. Furthermore, it is shown that sophisticated contact definitions (e.g. elastoplastic, asymptotic) can easily be incorporated into the existing algorithm.",
